= Theory of mind =

== Overview ==
'''Theory of mind''' is the capacity or set of processes used to represent, attribute, and reason about mental states such as beliefs, desires, intentions, knowledge, perceptions, and emotions.

The term does not require people to possess an explicit scientific theory of minds.

== Mental-state reasoning ==
Understanding behavior often requires distinguishing what another agent believes or wants from what is actually true or from one's own knowledge.

This ability supports prediction, explanation, communication, teaching, cooperation, and [[Pragmatics|pragmatic]] interpretation.

== False-belief tasks ==
False-belief tasks test whether a participant predicts behavior according to another person's mistaken belief.

They are historically important, especially in developmental research.

However:

<pre>
false-belief task performance ≠ theory of mind as a whole
</pre>

Language, memory, inhibition, attention, task pragmatics, and response demands can affect performance.

== Development ==
Explicit false-belief performance improves strongly in early childhood.

Research using looking-time and other nonverbal paradigms has produced claims of earlier sensitivity, but interpretation of those findings remains debated.

== Individual differences ==
Theory-of-mind performance varies with language, social experience, executive demands, task design, development, and clinical/neurodevelopmental factors.

A low score on one task should not be treated as a complete measure of a person's social understanding.

== Artificial systems ==
Language models and other AI systems can succeed on some theory-of-mind-like tasks.

Such performance does not by itself establish humanlike mental-state representations or mechanisms; evaluation can contain linguistic shortcuts and task artifacts.

== Related concepts ==
[[Pragmatics]], [[Common ground]], and [[Anthropomorphism]] connect theory-of-mind reasoning to communication and social attribution.

== References ==
